{
  "dom_id": "step-q114419",
  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14056389/assets/036e249b188b46fe94bd6955fce114cb11dc0f1e1c169fb58d36135cfbc9b797.png)\n\nThe function $g(x)$ is continuous and twice-differentiable for all $x.$ The graph $y={g}^{\u2033}(x)$ is shown above. At what value(s) of $x$ does $g(x)$ have a point of inflection?",
  "worked_solution": "First, notice that since $g(x)$ is twice-differentiable, the tangent line to the graph exists for all real $x.$\n\nInflection points occur when a graph changes concavity. In order for the graph to change concavity, the value of the second derivative ${g}^{\u2033}(x)$ must switch signs. So, we are looking for points where the graph of ${g}^{\u2033}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis.\n\nWe can see that the graph $y={g}^{\u2033}(x)$ does not cross the $x$-axis. \n\nTherefore, $g(x)$ has no inflection points.",
